Third semantic tracing expansion
================================

Scope and availability
----------------------
This implements an initial semantic surface in all 21 areas listed in
NEXT-COVERAGE, plus loader/security coverage, with 27 BSDInstruments examples. It does not claim that every
branch of these subsystems is instrumented. In particular, raw DNS/PAM/SSH
payloads, credentials, keys, file contents and command contents are absent
from the new events. Existing devd action arguments are unchanged.

coverage-third.json is the declaration/type/source inventory. A declaration
is not proof of a loaded or firing probe. Kernel providers require
KDTRACE_HOOKS; user providers require MK_DTRACE=yes and acquire PID suffixes
at runtime. The runtime linker registers its DOF after self relocation using
raw syscalls, without the ordinary libc-dependent drti constructor.

Conventions
-----------
Arguments below are positional (arg0 first). An address is an opaque identity,
not permission to dereference a saved object. Pair synchronous calls by PID,
thread and nesting depth; object addresses alone are reused and insufficient.
Pair swap and crypto across workers by live buffer/request identity. Begin
tracing before the workload. Attaching halfway through loses start events.

Ordinary returned failures have completion probes. Fatal exits, process
termination, panic, cancellation/nonlocal exits inside external callbacks,
and constructors that never return can leave unfinished pairs. Missing
completion never means success. Use proc:::exit and thread lifecycle probes
when investigating these cases. Threadwait records individual raw waits
before deferred cancellation is acted on, not the entire pthread API call.
No timestamps, allocation, locks or string formatting are added to feed probes.
Opt-in hidden DOF
anchors retain metadata in ordinary static-archive links; these add constant
pointer data/relocations, not hot-path calls. Retaining metadata can also
retain the other instrumented functions referenced by that provider.
Generated USDT enabled checks guard argument evaluation. Disabled build
macros discard arguments. Kernel SDT evaluates arguments only on its enabled
path on architectures supporting the patched static probe implementation.

Kernel contracts
----------------
tmpfs:
  vnode-start(mount,node,lock_flags), vnode-done(mount,node,errno).
  reclaim-wait(node), reclaim-resume(node): one reclaim msleep interval.
  resize-start(vnode,new_size), resize-done(vnode,errno).
  node-start(tmpfs_mount,vtype), node-done(tmpfs_mount,errno).
  capacity(tmpfs_mount,reason): 1=node limit, 2=page availability refusal.
  rename-start(source_directory,target_directory),
  rename-done(source_directory,relock_attempts,errno).
  Relock attempt count includes the first attempt. Completion identities
  are not dereferenced after the routine releases vnode references.
fat:
  alloc-start(mount,start_cluster,requested_clusters),
  alloc-done(mount,first_cluster,allocated_clusters,errno). Cluster outputs
  are zero on error; partial allocations do not imply requested count met.
  entry-start(mount,cluster,FAT_GET/FAT_SET mask), entry-done(mount,cluster,errno).
  integrity(mount); remount-start(mount), remount-done(mount,errno).
  Remount events surround actual vfs_remount_ro, not merely scheduling it.
nfsclient:
  recover-start(client), recover-done(client,last_local_or_NFS_status).
  The recovery done status is the last operation's status, NOT a summary
  proving all opens/locks/delegations were recovered; state may be discarded.
  setclient-result(client,status,attempts_remaining_before_decrement).
  renew-start(client), renew-done(client,status): primary lease renewal;
  additional pNFS data-server renewals are not included in this pair.
  open-start(vnode,access_mode), open-done(vnode,status): local open-state
  preparation, not the whole wire OPEN exchange.
  lock-release(lock_owner,status,can_delete): before possible owner free.
nfsserver:
  compound-start(request,minor_version),
  compound-done(request,returned_operations,NFS_status,local_error).
  operation-start(request,opcode),
  operation-done(request,opcode,NFS_status,local_error).
  Internal ERELOOKUP retries remain within one operation interval.
  client-start(request,client_operation_flags), client-done(request,status).
  session-replay(request): cached CREATE_SESSION sequence detected.
  Request identity can be NULL in client management; thread/depth still pair.
swap:
  io-start(buffer,read_write_command,requested_bytes),
  io-done(buffer,read_write_command,residual_bytes,errno).
  Completion precedes buffer release. Synchronous writes invoke the same
  completion path after bwait; their duration includes waiter scheduling.
vm:
  inactive-start(domain,shortage), inactive-done(domain,initial_shortage,
  remaining_shortage). Shortages are scan accounting, not physical page-I/O
  counts; negative values must be interpreted as signed integers.
iommu:
  map-start(domain,size,flags), map-done(domain,errno): gas-map reservation
  and mapping function, including address-space and page-table failures.
  unload-queue(tag,map): entry to unload request (may execute inline).
  unload-start(domain), unload-done(domain): one deferred unload worker batch,
  not one request and not hardware invalidation acknowledgement.
  load-complete(map,segment_count,errno): busdma completion boundary before
  reacquiring the driver's lock. Correlate queued work separately.
acpi:
  device-start(device,operation), device-done(device,operation,errno), where
  operation 0=suspend and 1=resume at the native ACPI bus boundary.
  prepare-start(device,sleep_type), prepare-done(device,sleep_type,ACPI_STATUS).
  sleep-start(softc,sleep_type), sleep-done(softc,sleep_type,ACPI_STATUS).
  Do not interpret clock deltas across suspend as calibrated asleep time.
crypto:
  submit(request,driver), defer(request), invoke-start(request,driver,hint),
  invoke-done(request,driver_return), complete(request,crypto_error),
  submit-error(request,immediate_driver_error).
  Submit is emitted once per dispatch, including asynchronous workqueue
  dispatch. Driver ERESTART may cause multiple invokes and queueing.
  Complete is emitted before callback dispatch; the callback may run and
  free/resubmit the request BEFORE invoke-done. Never read the request at
  invoke-done or equate driver return with asynchronous completion.

Userspace contracts (provider names below omit their PID suffix)
---------------------------------------------------------------
nameres:
  query-start(resolver_state,query_bytes,answer_capacity),
  query-done(resolver_state,returned_length,errno_on_failure_else_zero).
  server-start(resolver_state,server_index,tcp),
  server-done(resolver_state,server_index,length_or_zero_or_minus_one,
  last_transport_error_on_nonpositive_result_else_zero).
  TCP fallback and retries produce separate server attempts. This is
  res_nsend transport coverage, not resolver-cache hits above it.
  dispatch-start(database_name_pointer,method_name_pointer),
  dispatch-done(database_name_pointer,NSS_status).
  backend-start(database_name_pointer,backend_name_pointer),
  backend-done(database_name_pointer,backend_name_pointer,NSS_status).
  Backend pairs cover direct method calls, not special cache/fallback helpers;
  enclosing dispatch pairs include those paths. NSS statuses are bitmasks.
threadwait:
  mutex-start(mutex), mutex-done(mutex,error): contended lock slow path.
  condition-start(condition,kernel_path), condition-done(condition,raw_error).
  kernel_path is 1 for kernel condition waits and 0 for user-queue sleeps.
  join-start(target_thread), join-done(target_thread,raw_wait_error).
  Raw waits may return EINTR before cancellation or error normalization.
  A single API call can contain multiple intervals; mutex reacquisition
  after a condition wait is outside that condition interval.
rtldtrace:
  load-start(name_pointer,flags), load-done(name_pointer,object_or_zero).
  Includes cache hits and failures; name can be NULL for descriptor loads.
  relocate-start(first_object), relocate-done(first_object,result).
  init-start(object), init-done(object): initializer group while object held.
  Constructors can recursively dlopen; use nesting, not a single timestamp.
  Registration failure (absent helper device) is ignored, preserving errno.
  Linker startup before self relocation is intentionally not probed.
pamchain:
  chain-start(PAM_handle,primitive), chain-done(PAM_handle,PAM_result).
  module-start(PAM_handle,chain_element,control_flag),
  module-done(PAM_handle,chain_element,PAM_result).
  Missing entry points report PAM_SYMBOL_ERR; PAM_IGNORE is visible before
  being skipped. Control flags and chain result explain short-circuiting.
fetchio:
  request-start(URL_identity), request-done(URL_identity,stream_ready,
  fetchLastErrCode_on_failure_else_zero).
  Request completion means response headers/stream readiness, not body EOF.
  connect-start(host_pointer,port), connect-done(host_pointer,connected).
  tls-start(connection), tls-done(connection,result).
  Host and URL arguments are identities; examples do not copy their strings.
archiveio:
  operation-start(archive,operation,requested_bytes),
  operation-done(archive,operation,result).
  Operations: 1=write_header, 2=write_data, 3=write_data_block,
  4=read_next_header, 5=read_next_header2, 6=read_data_block,
  7=write_finish_entry, 8=write_close, 9=read_close.
  Only operation 2 returns a byte count. Other operations report ARCHIVE_*
  status (including operation 3 despite its la_ssize_t return type).
  Requested bytes are supplied for 2 and 3, zero otherwise. Internal vtable
  calls bypassing these public wrappers do not generate these events.
dhcplife:
  state(interface,state_entry): 1=reboot, 2=init, 3=selecting, 4=bound,
  5=panic. This records state-handler entry, not lease success.
  script-start(interface), script-done(interface,helper_result): privileged
  IPC round trip including child execution, but fatal IPC errors do not return.
devd:
  event-start(event_type), event-done(event_type); rule(rule_identity,matched).
  action-start(action_identity), action-done(action_identity,wait_status).
  child(pid): parent fork success. Action result is wait4 status or -1,
  not the boolean returned by action::do_action. No new command contents.
ffscheck: phase-start(phase), phase-done(phase), phases 1..5. Completion means
  the pass returned, not that the filesystem was clean or fully repaired.
fatcheck: check-start(filename_pointer), check-done(filename_pointer,fsck_exit).
mountop: operation-start(spec_pointer), operation-done(spec_pointer,result).
umountop: operation-start(statfs_pointer), operation-done(statfs_pointer,result).
  Mount/umount results are existing helper return conventions, not errno.
exports: reload-start(pass), reload-done(pass): export-list reload pass,
  including its partial failures; done does not assert every export applied.
logrotate:
  rotate-start(config), rotate-done(config,FREE_ENT_or_KEEP_ENT). This is a
  config ownership result, NOT a rotation success code; compression is later.
  compress-start(work,child_pid), compress-done(work,child_pid,wait_status,
  wait_errno). wait_status is -1 on wait failure; errno zero otherwise.
  The pair measures parent wait, not fork/exec setup or post-compression chmod.
  notify(work,signal,errno_or_zero): actual kill result, not daemon reopen ack.
timediscipline:
  select-start(previous_peer), select-done(selected_peer,survivors).
  Peer identity can change across selection; pair by thread/depth.
  adjust-start(peer), adjust-done(peer,local_clock_result).
  These expose selection and discipline processing, not a guarantee that
  the operating system accepted every adjustment; no double ABI arguments.
sshkeys:
  newkeys-start(connection,initial_exchange), newkeys-done(connection,SSH_error).
  These measure NEWKEYS processing, not the entire key exchange.
  channel-new(connection,local_channel_number,channel_type),
  channel-free(connection,local_channel_number),
  channel-reply(connection,confirmation_not_failure,parser_result).
  Channel reply result zero means the packet was processed, not acceptance
  when confirmation_not_failure=0. No channel payload/command strings.
sshauth:
  method-start(connection,attempt), method-done(connection,attempt,authenticated).
  decision(connection,attempt,authenticated,partial,postponed) records the
  method/authorization-list decision before optional PAM account checks and
  reply transmission. It is deliberately NOT a final login-success event.
  Connection addresses are per-process identities. Do not merge them across
  the monitor/auth/session privilege-separation boundary. Method can postpone;
  a later decision may arrive without another synchronous method call.

Examples and verification
-------------------------
Run bsdinstruments generate -f /usr/share/bsdinstruments/profiles/<name>.d
(and optionally --pid PID) to render an example. Run the rendered script with
DTrace on a kernel that exposes the required providers. Profile selectors and
argument bounds are checked against coverage-third.json before offline
compilation; -Z alone is not evidence that probes exist.

Use coverage-third-compile.py with a prepared GENERIC-NODEBUG object tree for
kernel enabled/disabled compilation. coverage-third-profile-check.py renders
and offline-compiles all 27 profiles with and without a PID filter. Native
userspace validation builds both modes and links provider objects into the
actual libraries/executables. Runtime workloads and availability evidence are
recorded separately in the validation report; absent live DTrace is not
reported as a passing runtime tracing test.

Current host runtime qualification
----------------------------------
The host exposes no DTrace device, so live enumeration/firing is unqualified.
The new source runtime linker also calls pre-existing cap_process (syscall
1011), which this running kernel does not implement. Default direct loader
execution raises SIGSYS in BOTH hook-enabled and disabled builds. The smoke
suite reports this as a host limitation, not a passing native execution test.
A separate, explicitly named host-compat run inherits SIGSYS ignored so the
existing ENOSYS fallback can run; both variants pass initial loading, nested
constructors/dlopen, dlclose, and a missing-library error in that mode. No host
signal policy, installed loader, kernel, or library is changed by these tests.

The ordinary smoke suite exercises shared and static libarchive (including
DOF retention in the static consumer), loopback HTTP success/404/connect/TLS
failure, contended pthread mutex/condition cancellation and join, and rotation
plus compression of disposable logs with daemon notification disabled. The
core smoke suite exercises invalid and loopback DNS queries, local NSS, and a
deny-only PAM policy. PAM policy-file verification is disabled only inside its
unprivileged test process to load the disposable policy; no installed policy
is edited. Run these only against matching source-built library dependencies.

Build-rule regression checks
----------------------------
bsd.dep.mk now supplies NOSSPSOBJS to the .nossppico provider rule. Using SOBJS
there produced an empty -G input list and failed libc_nossp_pic.a. Libraries
that opt into DTRACE_STATIC_ANCHOR get a hidden __bsd_dof_anchor_<file> symbol;
probe wrappers retain a reference so archive extraction keeps their DOF.
Existing providers do not opt in automatically. The rtld executable links
its lazy DOF directly and does not need an archive anchor. Test executables
verify the .SUNW_dof section is present only in hook-enabled variants.

Loader, debugger, MAC Framework and Capsicum extension (28 declarations)
-----------------------------------------------------------------------
rtldtrace dlopen-start(name pointer, fd, mode), dlopen-done(name pointer,
fd, mode, returned handle) encompass rtld_dlopen, including constructors.
The name is the caller-requested library string, not a canonical path.
NULL name and fd -1 means dlopen(NULL); fdlopen has NULL name and its fd.
Read the name using copyinstr at start; library-loads copies and retains it
per nested call. NULL result means failure; RTLD_TRACE/process exit or a
nonlocal exit need not complete. dlclose-start(handle), dlclose-done(handle,
status) cover the public call, including invalid handles. Handles at done
are identity-only; the referenced object may already have been unloaded.

ptrace request-start(request, pid, descriptor mode, process descriptor),
request-done(same, errno) encompass ptrace_action, including early errors,
PT_TRACE_ME, PT_ATTACH, PT_DETACH and internal Linux seize operations.
The request and target selectors are the originals, before normalization;
PID is not necessarily a resolved target (descriptor mode and TRACE_ME).
Existing attach, detach and deny events retain their original contracts.
This traces debugger requests, not kernel debugger entry or physical JTAG.

mac_framework hook-start(hook name, policy name), hook-done(same, raw return,
kind) surround actual calls in CHECK, GRANT, BOOLEAN and PERFORM dispatch,
including sleeping and non-sleeping forms, init/destroy and policy syscalls.
kind 1 denotes an integer result (boolean/grant/check meanings differ);
kind 0 denotes a void callback, whose zero status is not an access decision.
String arguments are kernel strings. Boolean short-circuit evaluation and
policy ordering are preserved; a skipped callback emits no event. Existing
combined-check and individual policy-decision events remain available.
Names cover label initialization/destruction, copy, relabel, internalize,
externalize, notification and authorization hooks using these dispatchers.
register-start(policy pointer), register-done(policy pointer, errno), and
unregister-start/unregister-done include refusals and no-op unregisters.
Existing mac:policy events still identify successful lifecycle operations.
mac_label init/destroy/free(label), alloc(label or NULL, allocation flags),
set(label, slot, new scalar value) expose framework label lifetime and slot
writes. Values may be policy-private pointer identities; do not dereference
saved labels after destruction. Labels embedded in mbufs have init/destroy
without necessarily having zone alloc/free events.

capsicum limit-start(operation name, fd), limit-done(same, errno) cover native
rights, ioctls, fcntls, xfer, xfer_rights, xfer_ioctls, xfer_fcntls, cloexec
and clofork limit syscall implementations, including copyin/validation errors.
Existing specific limit events remain. xfer-rights-start(fd, rights pointer),
xfer-rights-done(same, errno), xfer-ioctls-start(fd, requested count),
xfer-ioctls-done(same, errno), xfer-fcntls-start(fd, mask) and
xfer-fcntls-done(same, errno) also cover shared kernel helpers and compat
callers. No ioctl-array pointer is exposed after its ownership changes.
check-allow(have rights pointer, need rights pointer, violation type) fills
the successful-check gap beside existing check-deny. ioctl-check(fd, command,
errno) and fcntl-check(filedescent pointer, command, errno) report both outcomes.
Descriptor and rights pointers are valid only within their probe context.
Existing fd xfer-deny, xfer-consume, scm-rights-send and scm-rights-recv already
cover descriptor passing; these are not duplicated by this extension.
No claim is made that these are every Capsicum enforcement site.

New profiles: library-loads, debugger-requests, mac-hooks, capsicum-limits.
The batch now adds 151 declarations (79 kernel and 72 userspace).

Validation record (2026-10-11)
------------------------------
72 kernel translation-unit compiles (36 with/without hooks), including every
MAC framework C file, pass with -Werror. The GENERIC-NODEBUG kernel links.
All 36 disabled kernel objects lack SDT probe/provider references.
36 userspace artifacts have the expected DOF enabled/disabled state; the
additional static PAM builds pass in both modes. All 52 profile render and
offline compile checks pass. MAC dispatch fixtures using the actual header
macros verify evaluation count, policy order, return composition, boolean
short-circuiting and balanced locks with and without probe expansions.
Loader startup, nested initializers, dlopen/dlclose and missing-library tests
pass under the explicit host compatibility run described above. Ordinary
loader execution and live DTrace qualification remain blocked as documented.
Machine-readable results: /usr/obj/5bsd-dtrace-third-validation/validation.json.
Source inventory: 1862 explicit nonfixture declarations (1210 kernel, 652
userspace); this excludes dynamic providers and macro-generated declarations.
Three expansion batches add 307 declarations in total (95 + 61 + 151).

MAC hook enhancement
--------------------
MAC-HOOKS documents the added hook-args and hook-skip contracts, per-callback
argument type mapping and the mac-hook-arguments profile. This adds two kernel
declarations and one profile: third-batch totals are now 153 declarations
(81 kernel, 72 userspace) and 27 profiles, or 309 additions across three batches.
